      "provenance": "literature_retrieved",
      "rationale": "L2 explicitly states the HA epitope at position 1001 is on the extracellular side of the plasma membrane by IF in intact cells, and L1 confirms the tagged protein confers etoposide resistance. Python lookup of the numbered sequence confirms residue 1001 = D and residue 1002 = D, both within the extracellular span 993-1022. This is the best-evidenced site: longest extracellular loop, furthest from TM boundaries, dual validation (surface + function). Full-length MRP1/ABCC1 with HA epitope inserted at position 1001, expressed in HeLa cells. The extracellular loop 993-1022 (MSD3) is 30 residues long; position 1001 is 9 residues from the proximal TM boundary (992) and 22 from the distal (1023). N1006 is a glycosylation site 5 residues downstream.",
      "residue_after": "D",
      "residue_before": "D",
      "residue_label": "D1001",
      "residue_range": null,
      "site_id": "ABCC1-internal-1001-lit",
      "site_kind": "internal",
      "source_tier": "paper",
      "sources": [
        {
          "pmid": 9485377,
          "citation": "PMID 9485377",
          "claim": "The intracellular versus extracellular location of the HA epitopes present in biologically active MRP variants was then established in intact and permeabilized cells by immunofluorescence using an anti-HA antibody. Epitopes inserted at positions 1001 and 1222 were located on the extracellular side of the plasma membrane, while the epitope inserted at position 938 was located intracellularly. These results are consistent with a six TM rather than a four TM domain model for the membrane portion of the carboxy-terminal half of MRP.",
          "title": "Epitope insertion favors a six transmembrane domain model for the carboxy-terminal portion of the multidrug resistance-associated protein",
          "authors": "Kast & Gros",
